1
00:00:09,275 --> 00:00:10,710
[classical music playing over radio]

2
00:00:10,777 --> 00:00:12,612
[engine revving]

3
00:00:27,293 --> 00:00:31,731
[Jerry] This is a 1956 Chevrolet Corvette
convertible in cascade green,

4
00:00:32,565 --> 00:00:35,635
with beige coves and interior.

5
00:00:36,102 --> 00:00:39,139
It’s a pretty rare color,
only 290 were made.

6
00:00:40,974 --> 00:00:45,578
It has a 265-cubic inch,
240 horsepower V-8.

7
00:00:45,766 --> 00:00:47,433
Its got whitewall tires.

8
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:50,733
A two-speed Powerglide
automatic transmission.

9
00:00:50,784 --> 00:00:53,053
Signal seeking AM radio.

10
00:00:54,233 --> 00:01:00,099
It is absolutely the essence
of American futuristic positivism

11
00:01:00,160 --> 00:01:02,796
of the post-war society at that time.

12
00:01:04,197 --> 00:01:08,568
In the 1950s, people just didn’t see
anything that was bad.

13
00:01:08,768 --> 00:01:12,672
Everything, from the American perspective,
looked like this car.

14
00:01:13,239 --> 00:01:18,078
Colorful, energetic, happy...
"We’ve perfected life."

15
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:22,300
They couldn’t have been more wrong,
of course, but still.

16
00:01:22,348 --> 00:01:23,950
I thought this would be perfect...

17
00:01:24,017 --> 00:01:25,418
That car is you.

18
00:01:25,485 --> 00:01:26,886
...for my special guest this week.

19
00:01:27,187 --> 00:01:28,154
This is great.

20
00:01:28,221 --> 00:01:33,626
[Jerry] The irrepressibly enjoyable host 
of NBC’s Tonight Show, Mr. Jimmy Fallon.

21
00:01:34,661 --> 00:01:37,597
When you’re around Jimmy,
you’re having a good time.

22
00:01:37,664 --> 00:01:38,731
I don’t care who you are.

23
00:01:38,798 --> 00:01:40,033
[cell phone ringing]

24
00:01:40,133 --> 00:01:41,399
-[Jimmy] Hello?
-[Jerry] Jimmy?

25
00:01:41,468 --> 00:01:42,936
-[Jimmy] This it?
-[Jerry] We’re gonna get coffee.

26
00:01:43,236 --> 00:01:46,005
The talk show that comes to your house
and picks you up.

27
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:51,678
Hi, I’m Jerry Seinfeld,

28
00:01:51,744 --> 00:01:54,848
and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

29
00:01:54,981 --> 00:01:56,749
[engine revving]

30
00:01:58,051 --> 00:01:59,319
[brake squeaks]

31
00:01:59,853 --> 00:02:02,455
What car just makes you smile
and want to have fun?

32
00:02:02,522 --> 00:02:04,824
An aqua ’56 Vette.

33
00:02:05,758 --> 00:02:07,660
-That’s a beautiful thing.
-It’s beautiful.

34
00:02:07,727 --> 00:02:10,497
This is just gorgeous, and the color.
What happened with cars?

35
00:02:10,563 --> 00:02:12,932
Where did they stop making
colors like this?

36
00:02:12,999 --> 00:02:14,167
Why did people

37
00:02:14,234 --> 00:02:16,469
-stop having fun. Why?
-Stop having fun. Why?

38
00:02:16,536 --> 00:02:19,873
In the larger sense of life.
Why did people stop having fun?

39
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:21,741
Why did they stop buying trampolines?

40
00:02:21,808 --> 00:02:23,276
That’s a fantastic thing.

41
00:02:23,343 --> 00:02:25,078
Everybody used to have them in the '50s.

42
00:02:25,144 --> 00:02:27,046
-Oh, my God.
-[engine revving]

43
00:02:27,113 --> 00:02:29,549
Are you kidding me? [laughing]

44
00:02:29,749 --> 00:02:31,551
Car’s good, I’m good. Car’s bad, I’m bad.

45
00:02:31,618 --> 00:02:33,553
That’s the way I live.
‘Cause I’m a car guy.

46
00:02:36,689 --> 00:02:38,091
[Jimmy] This is it, we’re moving.

47
00:02:38,458 --> 00:02:41,127
This is great. Oh, this feels good.

48
00:02:41,194 --> 00:02:43,696
This is...
It doesn’t even feel like a convertible.

49
00:02:43,763 --> 00:02:45,298
I know. It feels like a set.

50
00:02:45,365 --> 00:02:46,666
-Like a spaceship.
-Yeah.

51
00:02:46,733 --> 00:02:48,001
It feels like we’re on the set

52
00:02:48,200 --> 00:02:50,633
and it’s just like a fake screen
next to you.

53
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:51,538
-Right?
-Right.

54
00:02:51,604 --> 00:02:53,540
And next to you.
It doesn’t feel like the real thing.

55
00:02:53,633 --> 00:02:55,266
-This is a Quentin Tarantino movie.
-Yeah.

56
00:02:55,308 --> 00:02:59,312
I wish I had a blood packet I could
just start throwing right here, right now.

57
00:03:03,583 --> 00:03:06,019
It’s interesting to hang out
with other comedians.

58
00:03:06,085 --> 00:03:07,186
-Yes.
-Don’t you find,

59
00:03:07,253 --> 00:03:10,156
'cause I've found this, that every time
you go to a party,

60
00:03:10,223 --> 00:03:11,758
wherever, whatever party it is,

61
00:03:11,824 --> 00:03:13,459
if there’s another comedian there,

62
00:03:13,533 --> 00:03:17,133
I’m immediately going right in the corner
with the comedian and I hang out,

63
00:03:17,197 --> 00:03:18,598
-and I talk the whole party.
-Yeah.

64
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,602
Did you see some of those scripts,

65
00:03:22,669 --> 00:03:24,537
I think the Springsteen song,

66
00:03:24,604 --> 00:03:30,743
and was it a Dylan song that just
were auctioned, the original writing.

67
00:03:30,810 --> 00:03:34,080
Does that stuff interest you
as something... "I gotta have that"?

68
00:03:34,299 --> 00:03:36,066
No, but I mean
I love looking at that stuff.

69
00:03:36,115 --> 00:03:37,784
-I don't--
-I don’t know what I would do with it.

70
00:03:37,850 --> 00:03:41,955
You wouldn’t buy Born to Run
for $200,000 or something?

71
00:03:42,322 --> 00:03:43,523
-No.
-No. Why not?

72
00:03:43,590 --> 00:03:45,992
No, because Bruce Springsteen
wouldn’t buy it for $200,000.

73
00:03:46,059 --> 00:03:47,694
-He’s the one that wrote it.
-He wrote it.

74
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,262
Why would he ask? If he doesn’t want it,

75
00:03:49,600 --> 00:03:51,399
what am I gonna do with it?
If Bruce Spring--

76
00:03:51,466 --> 00:03:52,433
You're doing me again.

77
00:03:52,498 --> 00:03:54,601
[laughing] I can’t, I guess.
[speaking indistinctly]

78
00:03:54,899 --> 00:03:56,899
[Jerry] He doesn’t want it,
why do I want it?

79
00:03:56,936 --> 00:03:59,672
[Jimmy imitating Jerry] He wrote
Born to Run. He doesn’t want it!

80
00:03:59,739 --> 00:04:01,674
What am I gonna do with it?
I’m stuck with it.

81
00:04:01,741 --> 00:04:04,043
It’s not who wants it, it’s who gets stuck
with it.

82
00:04:04,110 --> 00:04:05,244
No, you take it!

83
00:04:07,013 --> 00:04:10,183
-What I remember about meeting you at SNL.
-[Jimmy laughing]

84
00:04:10,250 --> 00:04:13,519
I remember seeing you there,
when I did the show in the late '90s.

85
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:16,433
You were like a light bulb
that was just suspended in midair.

86
00:04:16,489 --> 00:04:17,991
That's what you looked like.

87
00:04:18,366 --> 00:04:20,133
You would just meet this guy, and you go,

88
00:04:20,193 --> 00:04:22,595
"Hey, there’s no wires
holding this light bulb up.

89
00:04:22,666 --> 00:04:26,433
And there’s no electricity to light it up.
How is this floating?"

90
00:04:26,499 --> 00:04:28,901
"That’s Jimmy Fallon.
He’s just excited to be here."

91
00:04:28,968 --> 00:04:31,204
-It’s just so...
-[both laughing]

92
00:04:32,839 --> 00:04:33,973
You know what I miss?

93
00:04:34,066 --> 00:04:36,733
I miss being an opening act
in front of like...

94
00:04:36,776 --> 00:04:39,312
I’d go out in front of Frankie Valli,
people going, "Who is this?

95
00:04:39,379 --> 00:04:41,180
We don’t want to see him,
we want to see Frankie Valli."

96
00:04:41,533 --> 00:04:42,766
And you would just go,

97
00:04:42,815 --> 00:04:44,150
-"What about this?
-You miss that?

98
00:04:44,217 --> 00:04:45,251
What about this joke?

99
00:04:45,318 --> 00:04:47,720
Why are they putting pictures
of the criminals on the walls

100
00:04:47,800 --> 00:04:48,833
of the post office?

101
00:04:48,900 --> 00:04:51,433
Why don’t they hang on to this guy
when they’re taking his picture?"

102
00:04:51,491 --> 00:04:53,426
-[laughing] Yeah.
-And then they go,

103
00:04:53,493 --> 00:04:56,462
"That is funny. That’s pretty funny."

104
00:04:56,529 --> 00:04:59,699
That feeling, you’ll never have
that feeling again,

105
00:04:59,766 --> 00:05:02,335
of, "Who the hell is Jimmy Fallon?
Why is Jimmy Fallon here?"

106
00:05:03,803 --> 00:05:04,871
Wow, that’s a really cool--

107
00:05:04,937 --> 00:05:06,072
That’s a cool old Caddie.

108
00:05:07,633 --> 00:05:08,666
-[Jimmy] It's--
-Of course.

109
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:10,933
-It’s an original driver.
-Yeah, original driver.

110
00:05:10,977 --> 00:05:13,179
-[laughing]
-Comes with original driver.

111
00:05:13,246 --> 00:05:14,981
-Comes with the original.
-Yeah.

112
00:05:21,187 --> 00:05:23,556
This is what I do. That’s my boat...

113
00:05:23,623 --> 00:05:25,191
-What?
-...and that’s my little truck.

114
00:05:25,258 --> 00:05:27,060
And I’m going to take you out
on it right now.

115
00:05:27,126 --> 00:05:27,994
-No.
-Yeah.

116
00:05:28,061 --> 00:05:29,829
And I’m going to put it
in the water myself.

117
00:05:29,896 --> 00:05:31,364
[laughs]

118
00:05:31,431 --> 00:05:32,732
-No way.
-Yeah, I know.

119
00:05:32,799 --> 00:05:33,933
Are you kidding me?

120
00:05:34,267 --> 00:05:36,336
This is unbelievable. This is awesome.

121
00:05:36,669 --> 00:05:37,704
-Yeah.
-Come on.

122
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:41,900
[Jerry] This is a 1994
 Land Rover Defender 90

123
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:43,733
that happens to be my personal car.

124
00:05:43,810 --> 00:05:46,379
I bought it when I was doing
my TV series in LA.

125
00:05:46,979 --> 00:05:50,450
I found it relaxing to get in this thing
and drive into a pile of dirt.

126
00:05:50,683 --> 00:05:53,186
I love these vehicles that seem like
they want to be punished.

127
00:05:55,121 --> 00:05:56,022
[horn blares]

128
00:05:56,089 --> 00:05:59,192
That was the boat I wanted when I was 15.
I could never get the money together.

129
00:05:59,258 --> 00:06:00,460
This is very exciting for me.

130
00:06:00,526 --> 00:06:01,794
This might be the thing.

131
00:06:01,861 --> 00:06:03,830
A boat, is it hard to take care of a boat?

132
00:06:03,896 --> 00:06:05,064
Not a little boat like this.

133
00:06:05,131 --> 00:06:06,866
And do you have to have your license
and--

134
00:06:06,933 --> 00:06:08,501
No, nothing. It’s like a slide whistle.

135
00:06:08,568 --> 00:06:09,602
Anyone can buy one.

136
00:06:11,070 --> 00:06:15,508
This is a 13-foot Boston Whaler,
the classic boat from the classic company.

137
00:06:18,544 --> 00:06:20,546
Boston Whaler began in 1958,

138
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:22,782
the boats were invented
by a guy named Dick Fischer.

139
00:06:24,317 --> 00:06:25,952
It has a blue gel coat interior,

140
00:06:26,018 --> 00:06:28,254
mahogany wood and a white steering wheel.

141
00:06:28,900 --> 00:06:31,500
I think it’s the greatest boat
in the world. I love the logo.

142
00:06:31,566 --> 00:06:34,700
I used to draw it in my schoolbooks,
just dreaming of getting one someday.

143
00:06:35,027 --> 00:06:36,929
This is everything
I ever wanted in a boat.

144
00:06:37,263 --> 00:06:40,366
Another absolutely indestructible vehicle.

145
00:06:40,733 --> 00:06:44,737
Here they are cutting the boat in half
to prove that it’s not just a slogan,

146
00:06:44,837 --> 00:06:49,475
the Boston Whaler
really is the unsinkable legend!

147
00:06:49,542 --> 00:06:51,244
[chainsaw buzzing]

148
00:06:52,545 --> 00:06:54,847
Oh, I love this.
I’m so excited about this.

149
00:06:56,949 --> 00:06:59,685
The reason I wanted to do it,
is because I know

150
00:07:00,319 --> 00:07:02,355
you, watching me...

151
00:07:03,133 --> 00:07:05,633
load this boat in the water
is going to make you laugh.

152
00:07:06,058 --> 00:07:07,426
["Spirit in the Sky" playing]

153
00:07:07,493 --> 00:07:10,196
Norman Greenbaum, you know,
the guy who wrote "Spirit in the Sky"?

154
00:07:10,266 --> 00:07:11,433
I love "Spirit in the Sky."

155
00:07:11,497 --> 00:07:12,465
Great song. He goes,

156
00:07:12,532 --> 00:07:16,235
"I have a home, and I have a summer home.
And I have a little boat."

157
00:07:16,866 --> 00:07:18,333
I wrote one song.

158
00:07:18,566 --> 00:07:19,900
It’s a Jesus song.

159
00:07:19,939 --> 00:07:21,774
What’s the lyrics? 
"Jesus" is in the lyrics.

160
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:24,210
-"I got a friend in Jesus."
-Yeah, "I got a friend in Jesus."

161
00:07:24,577 --> 00:07:27,246
♪ Gotta have a friend in Jesus ♪

162
00:07:27,313 --> 00:07:28,915
And the guy’s name is Greenbaum.

163
00:07:28,981 --> 00:07:30,983
[Jimmy laughing]

164
00:07:31,050 --> 00:07:35,321
I need more information.
I need more information.

165
00:07:35,388 --> 00:07:39,559
♪ He's gonna recommend you
To the spirit in the sky ♪

166
00:07:39,625 --> 00:07:42,895
♪That's where you're gonna go
When you die ♪

167
00:07:42,966 --> 00:07:44,666
[Jerry] This is not
where I launch my boat.

168
00:07:44,766 --> 00:07:46,566
But, uh, I'll figure it out.

169
00:07:46,632 --> 00:07:48,000
Oh, this going to be interesting.

170
00:07:50,269 --> 00:07:51,504
[Jerry] I don’t know where you’re going.

171
00:07:52,766 --> 00:07:54,299
But this not where I go.

172
00:07:57,400 --> 00:07:59,833
Yeah. I thought you had another way.

173
00:08:00,400 --> 00:08:03,366
All right. I’ll turn around
with this boat.

174
00:08:04,083 --> 00:08:07,286
[laughing]

175
00:08:07,666 --> 00:08:09,533
That was perfectly delivered.

176
00:08:09,633 --> 00:08:12,833
"All right, I’ll turn around,
with this boat."

177
00:08:15,561 --> 00:08:18,698
-That’s a lot of math, a lot of angles.
-This is math and angles and geometry.

178
00:08:18,764 --> 00:08:20,132
And so is comedy, by the way.

179
00:08:21,233 --> 00:08:23,033
Comedy's all angles and proofs.

180
00:08:23,069 --> 00:08:24,804
-Remember writing proofs?
-Yeah, of course.

181
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:25,705
That's stand-up.

182
00:08:25,771 --> 00:08:29,041
You say something ridiculous,
and then you prove it to be true.

183
00:08:30,142 --> 00:08:32,011
Why are the Raisin Bran people

184
00:08:32,078 --> 00:08:34,113
constantly telling us about the scoops?

185
00:08:34,280 --> 00:08:35,448
That’s my premise.

186
00:08:35,900 --> 00:08:39,799
Now we got more scoops, bigger scoops,
longer scoops, all raisins, one flake.

187
00:08:39,866 --> 00:08:43,633
We don’t like the cereal, okay?
We don’t like the cereal.

188
00:08:43,689 --> 00:08:45,858
-It doesn’t matter how many scoops.
-[Jimmy laughing]

189
00:08:48,694 --> 00:08:50,463
I, you know what, I think too,

190
00:08:50,566 --> 00:08:53,166
I mean, I can’t believe that
we keep buying new and improved.

191
00:08:53,232 --> 00:08:55,067
There’s new Mr. Clean, new and improved.

192
00:08:55,134 --> 00:08:58,070
-What was the swill that we were buying?
-Swill!

193
00:08:58,137 --> 00:09:02,375
When we originally invented Mr. Clean,
what was that crap?

194
00:09:02,441 --> 00:09:04,677
-Nobody’s drinking Mr. Clean. Swill.
-I know, but swill.

195
00:09:06,279 --> 00:09:08,981
[Jerry] When I did the speech
at the Clios, I was talking about,

196
00:09:09,048 --> 00:09:14,520
I don't care that you’re lying to us
in the ad and that the product stinks,

197
00:09:14,600 --> 00:09:17,933
because when I’m watching the commercial
and you say it’s great, I’m happy then.

198
00:09:18,000 --> 00:09:19,699
And when I go to get it, I’m happy.

199
00:09:19,766 --> 00:09:20,933
When I get it home, I’m happy,

200
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:23,533
and then I try and use it,
it doesn’t do it.

201
00:09:24,100 --> 00:09:25,100
And then, I’m okay.

202
00:09:25,197 --> 00:09:27,667
-‘Cause I’ll watch another commercial.
-You got a lot out of it.

203
00:09:27,733 --> 00:09:29,466
Yeah, you’re doing pretty good.

204
00:09:29,533 --> 00:09:31,233
You’re making us pretty happy.

205
00:09:31,733 --> 00:09:33,633
Because we’re getting
moments of happiness.

206
00:09:33,706 --> 00:09:35,675
That’s all I want.
I don’t want to be happy all the time.

207
00:09:35,741 --> 00:09:36,943
-Yep.
-Just now and then.

208
00:09:37,033 --> 00:09:39,000
That’s a lot of happy, though. Like now,

209
00:09:39,066 --> 00:09:40,966
sometimes, I don’t know
what commercials are,

210
00:09:41,013 --> 00:09:43,082
-‘cause now they’re all for drugs.
-Yeah.

211
00:09:43,149 --> 00:09:45,151
And I don’t know
'cause they’re people being happy.

212
00:09:45,217 --> 00:09:46,786
It makes you want it
because you want to be happy

213
00:09:46,852 --> 00:09:47,820
You want to be like them.

214
00:09:48,233 --> 00:09:51,166
They break up the drug commercials
with ads for law firms

215
00:09:51,223 --> 00:09:53,025
to sue for the drugs you took last year.

216
00:09:53,092 --> 00:09:54,327
That’s the only relief.

217
00:09:54,400 --> 00:09:55,533
"Do you ever get thirsty?"

218
00:09:55,595 --> 00:09:58,598
And you go, "I guess.
Yeah, I’ve been thirsty before.

219
00:09:58,664 --> 00:09:59,665
Do I need this?

220
00:10:00,166 --> 00:10:02,134
How am I living my life without this?"

221
00:10:02,866 --> 00:10:04,433
"Do you ever wake up in the morning?"

222
00:10:06,005 --> 00:10:09,275
Is that Batman there? Is that Rocket Man?
Who is that?

223
00:10:09,375 --> 00:10:11,043
They’re filming the new Batman.

224
00:10:11,110 --> 00:10:12,812
-Hey, go for it.
-All right.

225
00:10:13,033 --> 00:10:14,733
Here’s the fun thing about a Land Rover.

226
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:16,048
You can go wherever you want.

227
00:10:16,749 --> 00:10:18,050
It’s a Land Rover, baby.

228
00:10:18,918 --> 00:10:20,453
Oh, we went in the sand.

229
00:10:20,620 --> 00:10:22,254
I love how everything’s done with looks.

230
00:10:22,321 --> 00:10:24,056
I love that he’s looking at us
like we’re weird.

231
00:10:24,123 --> 00:10:26,492
[bleep] is going on?
The guy’s attached to ropes.

232
00:10:26,559 --> 00:10:29,629
I mean, this couldn’t be a more awkward.
We’re weird?

233
00:10:29,695 --> 00:10:30,863
He’s Rocket Man.

